itation_capacity repair must recheck the SAME\ndraft against all supplied original text and return a genuinely sufficient smaller\nproof, or partial/unresolved if no such proof exists. Never delete a necessary\npremise merely to fit a size limit, and never remove a counter to save space.\nFor an explicit summary_revision, its inherited-source retention contract takes\npriority over minimizing citations: keep every required old identity, and choose\nonly the minimal relevant NEW citations. Do not shrink an inherited proof to fit.\nEach support value asserts the WHOLE value of that variable, including set/record\nvariables. Multiple support observations are alternative proofs of the SAME complete\nvalue, not additive fragments. Combine applicable sources into one complete value\nand cite all its premises. The controller never unions sets or merges records. If the visible text\nonly establishes part of the requested value, return partial with value=null and\nexplain the supported fragment in reason. Do not claim completeness from one window.\nrequested_scopes[slot.variable] is that variable's authoritative local scope. Use its\nentity_scope and time_scope strings exactly only when that scope actually applies;\notherwise return unknown/irrelevant. output_contract is the terminal answer's scope,\nnot the scope of every historical fact or parent. A requested parent retains its own\ndeclared scope during terminal reading and audit. Never relabel historical evidence as\ncurrent/future evidence. required_scope, when present, is only a shorthand shared by\nALL requested_scopes. Actual parents must be supplied supported bindings with\ntheir versions; reproduce sources when joining documents. Sources may be combined\nacross documents, but list ALL joint premises. A source slice is not an independent fact.\nHonor authoritative source_role: user statements, assistant suggestions, documents\nand unknown headers differ. An assistant suggestion does not establish the user's\npreference without user evidence. Observation order is not calendar event time;\ntemporal scope needs cited explicit text or authoritative time metadata, never a\ndate guessed from message order. List sources for every object/time qualifier.\nContradiction challenges existing fact route IDs in retract_ids, with cited raw text;\nit never itself creates a false binding. Emit contrary supported values separately.\nOnly same-variable known_observations[].id values are legal retraction targets.\nIf no such route exists, no retraction is available; preserve contrary evidence as\nan independently supported value, a partial/unknown observation, or slot.unresolved\ndescriptions. Never invent a target or discard evidence. Other stances require retract_ids=[].\nUnknown, absence, unexamined text, anomalies without a concrete observation are not false.\nInclude every relevant counter-observation in this batch before declaring support.\nSlots include the local variables and already-bound related parents. Check new text\nfor counterevidence to those parents as well. An observation's actual parent versions\nrefer to that variable's own planned task inputs, never to itself. A checked parent\nwith no new observation may return an empty observations list.\nFor a rule variable, value is {variables,expression,constraints,rules_complete}; only\npreplanned condition demands are allowed. Instantiate thresholds ONLY from applicable\nsource text. Rules must preserve the fixed output mode; one_reason uses one_reason,\nall_failures uses failures. Conditions can use numeric observations with units and\ncomparisons, not a guessed deletion latency. Mark incomplete rule collections false.\nIf rules_completion_required is true, seek explicit applicable completeness evidence.\nWhen a sourced complete rule supersedes a known incomplete rule, cite its original\nsources, retract the superseded route with a contradiction observation, and return\nthe complete rule as a separate support observation; never silently overwrite it.\nA successful observation at one time does not establish a universal no-return rule.\nRepresent a witnessed violation as IF(violation,false,unbound_compliance), keeping\npositive compliance unknown until explicit adequate scoped evidence supports it.\nFor semantic terminal tasks return the supported final text and final_prediction in\nthis same response, using public options only here. No later Reader will run.\nWhen public options are supplied, also return semantic_answer with its exact typed\nJSON value in this same terminal response: the supported semantic value, NOT an\noption letter. Only final_prediction holds the option label. Unsupported conclusions\nhave null labels.\nRepair returns only failed observations/slots, retaining IDs; never delete an invalid\ncounter-observation. If a failed ID has conflicting duplicate observations, inspect\nALL supplied drafts and resolve that ID without discarding potential counterevidence.\nIf repair_scope supplies replacement_observations for a duplicated ID, return EACH\nreplacement ID exactly once. Those deterministic replacement IDs separate the\noriginal drafts; fix their validation errors without dropping any draft or counter.\nrepair_scope.failed includes the actual invalid observation and\nits validation feedback. Correct those drafts in place, preserving each requested ID.\nAn id=\"*\" failure authorizes repairing that variable's whole slot, including new\nunique observation IDs for genuine additional reviews; it never unfreezes rows.\nNamed failed IDs must each remain present, including an ID rejected as an\nunrequested addition in the previous response: it is now a required failed draft.\nCorrect that draft under the same ID or preserve sourced partial/unknown evidence;\ndo not delete it or add other IDs unless a wildcard or replacement list permits it.\nAlready validated observations are frozen and must not be restated or changed.\nIf repair_scope supplies required_counter_observations, preserve every original\ncounter's stance, cited sources, scopes, actual parents and retraction targets.\nAn envelope-format repair does not authorize reclassifying a valid counter as\nirrelevant or dropping the fact route it challenges.\nevidence_obligations are outstanding scoped issues from earlier reads, not new\nfacts. Their original sources MUST be examined even under a new query identity.\nTo discharge an issue, explicitly return its id in that variable's optional\nresolved_obligation_ids, leave no unresolved issue in the slot, and provide\nsupported/corrective/irrelevant observations reviewing its cited original sources.\nThe union of those settled observations' source_ids must cover EVERY source_ids\nentry of the offered obligation. Review coverage is not a claim that every source\nis a premise of a support: genuinely irrelevant or corrective original text may\nhave a separate sourced review. Never add unsupported citations, classify text\nautomatically, or label supporting evidence irrelevant merely to satisfy coverage.\nAn empty slot does not resolve an earlier issue. Do not assert hypothetical\nwhole-corpus exhaustiveness requirements; scoped sufficient evidence may resolve\na missing-evidence issue without having read every memory.\n\nWhen source_span_columns is present, source_spans contains row arrays. Match each\nrow position to source_span_columns to reconstruct every span object before\nreading. References to source_spans[].id or other fields mean those objects.\nThe id column holds source aliases (sN); doc_id holds the original document ID or,\nwhen source_documents is present, its metadata reference (dN), never source_ids.\n\n\nCURRENT PHASE: HISTORY EVIDENCE. Complete only the supplied historical task and\nrequested variable scopes. This is not the final response-selection stage.\nFor retrieval, extract concrete statements supported by the supplied original\nhistory. For an intermediate compose task, summarize those historical facts,\npreferences, constraints and changes using the supplied parent versions and\noriginal quotations. A history summary describes what the sources establish;\nit must not address the user, offer a new recommendation or draft a reply.\nThe original user utterance provides query context. It is not an instruction to\nanswer it at this stage or to retrieve an exact duplicate of what it already says.\nPublic response options are not available here. Do not invent, compare or refer\nto options A/B/C/D. Return exactly the slots object; no final_prediction or\nsemantic_answer fields belong in a history-evidence response.\nKeep each value within its declared type, entity and time scope. Preserve earlier\nand later attitudes, conditions and supporting quotations; a historical dislike\nmust not silently replace a later documented change. Assistant advice alone does\nnot establish an adopted user preference. Do not invent external catalogues,\navailability or past execution of a future suggestion. A partial observation must\ncite its actual clue. Use unresolved only for a specific condition still necessary\nto establish this requested historical value, not hypothetical exhaustive coverage.\nFor one text-summary variable, synthesize compatible query-relevant facts into one\nsufficient source-grounded whole summary; do not report each event as a different\ncomplete value of that variable. Multiple supports must be alternative sufficient\nproofs of the same whole value. Keep partial evidence, negative experiences and\nvalid counters explicit when a complete scoped summary is not established.\nPositive and negative experiences can both support a historical summary. A sourced\ndislike is useful history, not automatically a contradiction observation: that\nstance is reserved for challenging an identified existing fact route. Preserve\nthe difference between past enjoyment, later frustration and their conditions in\nthe value and citations; do not summarize them as an unconditional current interest.\nRe-read any supplied evidence obligations and their original sources explicitly;\ndo not drop a source or contradiction merely because a prior value is available.\nOnly a variable explicitly declaring evidence_semantics=\"personal_history_summary\"\npermits a source-grounded summary_revision. For compatible added history, preserve\nthe old and new facts, negative experiences and time conditions in the new summary;\nexplicitly name the current old summary routes and version as offered by the\nprotocol. Re-read and cite their original sources together with the new evidence.\nThis retires an old summary representation without asserting that its historical\nfacts were false. A genuine factual refutation remains a contradiction with its\nown sources and targets. Never use summary revision to hide or discard a counter,\nchange scope, choose an arbitrary latest value, or revise the terminal response.\nA personal_atomic_fact variable keeps one scoped factual value. Different sources\nmay independently support that same value; separate compatible events are not\nalternative whole values of an atomic fact. This declaration does not permit summary_revision.\n\nThis request is phase=history_evidence. Its ONLY top-level output field is slots. Do not return semantic_answer, final_prediction, audit_verdict, or any other top-level field, even null. Read or summarize the requested scoped historical evidence; do not write a conversational reply or invent candidate responses.\n\nAn explicitly declared personal_history_summary is a revisable representation of\nsourced history. Compatible additional facts do not contradict the older facts.\nOnly summary_revision_contracts authorizes this request to revise such a summary:\nthis permission exists only in phase=history_evidence. Terminal response selection\nand audit forbid summary_revision even for requested historical parent summaries.\nThey may still report genuine sourced counters or conservative partial/unresolved\nissues; the phase restriction never permits discarding contrary evidence.\nreturn a support observation with summary_revision={supersedes_route_ids,\nprevious_version,reason}, using exact offered route IDs and the current version.\nsummary_revision_contracts.required_source_ids covers replacing ALL offered\neligible_route_ids. When selecting only a subset, retain those selected routes'\nsources, their actual-parent source closure and relevant same-variable counters.\nStructured repair feedback gives the exact required set for that draft's targets.\nThis capability is optional, not a requirement to revise an unchanged summary.\nWith no new observation, return observations=[] unless an outstanding review\nrequires its original draft IDs and sources. For such an unchanged draft, the\nsame sourced support with a valid summary_revision is an idempotent recheck: it\nkeeps the existing routes, ancestry and version, and does not assert new evidence.\nRe-read ALL old and new original premises and relevant counterevidence; retain\nthem in the new support's source_ids and preserve their factual content, negative\nexperiences, conditions and temporal qualifications in the revised whole summary.\nsummary_revision_sources in repair feedback names exact source identities for\nthe selected revision targets, not counts. Re-read and retain EVERY\nrequired_source_ids entry in that feedback; new sources cannot\nreplace missing old identities even if the total citation count is unchanged.\nThe complete missing_source_ids list is structured feedback, not permission to\nfill citations automatically. If the originals actually conflict, return a\nsource-grounded counter or partial/unresolved evidence instead of a revision.\nIn failed_batch_reviews this feedback describes the retained original failed\nobservation; repair_scope describes the current attempted correction.\nretract_ids stays empty. This explicitly retires the older representation, not its\nfacts. Never use revision to hide a genuine contradiction, unavailable evidence,\nor a different scope/parent version. Ordinary facts and all terminal/audit variables\ncannot use summary_revision. If a complete compatible account is not supported,\nreturn sourced partial/unresolved instead. Multiple new supports must agree on\none whole value. Existing superseded_by links identify retired representations;\ntheir original evidence remains available. revoked holds actual counterevidence,\nwhich is never erased by a summary revision or ignored through its ancestry.\n\n\nReturn one complete JSON object matching this schema:\n{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"slots\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"oneOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"allOf\":[{\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\"]}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"const\":\"user\"},\"time_scope\":{\"const\":\"past\"}}}}],\"else\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"type\":\"null\"}}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"const\":\"support\"}}},\"properties\":{\"retract_ids\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"]}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}}}}},\"variable\":{\"const\":\"history\"}}}],\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"parent_versions\":{\"additionalProperties\":{\"type\":\"integer\"},\"type\":\"object\"},\"reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"retract_ids\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"source_ids\":{\"description\":\"Exact visible source_spans[].id aliases, such as s3; never document IDs or invented IDs.\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"time_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"value\":{}},\"required\":[\"id\",\"stance\",\"value\",\"source_ids\",\"entity_scope\",\"time_scope\",\"parent_versions\",\"retract_ids\",\"reason\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"unresolved\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"variable\":{\"enum\":[\"history\"],\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"variable\",\"observations\",\"unresolved\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"maxItems\":1,\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"}},\"required\":[\"slots\"],\"type\":\"object\"}"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\"original_question\":\"Can you suggest some new cooking techniques or recipes I might enjoy exploring?\",\"task\":{\"question\":\"What cooking techniques or recipes has the user already tried or expressed dislike for?\",\"output_slot\":\"history\",\"answer_type\":\"text\",\"inputs\":[],\"execution\":\"retrieval\"},\"grounded_subquestion\":\"What cooking techniques or recipes has the user already tried or expressed dislike for?\",\"request_context\":{\"query_identity\":\"6b851fdaf2776c9ab50beb2bc0179f18b593c528a85c72e952d6b120193a2f6d\",\"algorithm_version\":\"dagbt_residual_memory_v2\",\"program_identity\":\"a103a2ae4f6ab1bc53690d2ec31da352645527ca92fbff10792335d4dde417ff\",\"initial_dag_identity\":\"0d3abfcc7ead40f08d633fa98740d5cb23ca04f995c6c16b7fdd81f172970a39\"},\"variables\":{\"history\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"demand\":\"history\",\"evidence_semantics\":\"personal_history_summary\",\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"past\"}}},\"requested_slots\":[\"history\"],\"requested_scopes\":{\"history\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"past\"}},\"output_contract\":{\"mode\":\"semantic\",\"result_type\":\"text\",\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\",\"explanation\":\"semantic\"},\"program_revision\":0,\"rule_binding\":null,\"supported_parents\":{},\"known_observations\":[],\"source_spans\":[[\"s1\",\"d1\",0,99,\"Conversation message indices 93–94 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s2\",\"d1\",99,105,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s3\",\"d1\",105,432,\"User: I've been exploring my passion for food lately, especially cooking. Cooking has become a delightful escape for me, a means of expressing my creativity and bringing joy to others. The process of selecting fresh ingredients, experimenting with different flavors, and presenting a dish beautifully is incredibly fulfilling. \",\"user\",[93]],[\"s4\",\"d1\",432,769,\"I often find myself lost in the nuances of taste and texture, and it's rewarding to see how a simple meal can evoke such happiness in those who share it with me. Lately, I've been trying to perfect my skills in making different cuisines, from Italian to Thai, and it has opened up a whole new world of culinary traditions and techniques.\",\"user\",[93]],[\"s5\",\"d1\",769,782,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s6\",\"d1\",782,1087,\"Assistant: That's wonderful to hear! Cooking is indeed a powerful way to express oneself, as it combines art and science in such a remarkable way. The joy of transforming raw ingredients into a delicious meal not only satisfies hunger but can create unforgettable memories for you and those you cook for. \",\"assistant\",[94]],[\"s7\",\"d1\",1087,1447,\"It's fascinating how different cultures approach cooking, using unique ingredients and techniques passed down through generations, enriching our understanding of the world through food. Cooking can also serve as a gateway to mindfulness, allowing you to engage your senses fully in the act of preparing a meal, and creating a meditative space in your kitchen. \",\"assistant\",[94]],[\"s8\",\"d1\",1447,1754,\"Furthermore, the challenge of mastering new dishes enhances your culinary skills and builds confidence each time you step into the kitchen. I'm curious, what specific cuisines or dishes have you been exploring most recently, and have you discovered any favorite flavors or techniques that resonate with you?\",\"assistant\",[94]],[\"s9\",\"d2\",0,99,\"Conversation message indices 95–96 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s10\",\"d2\",99,105,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s11\",\"d2\",105,392,\"User: I even took a cooking class recently to learn new techniques and recipes, which was really enlightening! It was fascinating to explore not just the recipes but also the cultural background behind them, as many dishes come with rich histories that deepen our appreciation for food. \",\"user\",[95]],[\"s12\",\"d2\",392,791,\"The hands-on experience of preparing different cuisines has really expanded my palate and made me consider how flavors interact in cooking. I also got to meet some amazing fellow food enthusiasts who shared their own stories and tips during the class, creating a vibrant community focused on culinary arts. Overall, it was much more than a class; it felt like a journey into the heart of gastronomy.\",\"user\",[95]],[\"s13\",\"d2\",791,804,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s14\",\"d2\",804,1130,\"Assistant: That's great to hear! Cooking classes can really open up your culinary skills and provide a wonderful opportunity to connect with others who share your passion for food. They often introduce not only various cooking techniques but also essential tips for utilizing ingredients you might not have considered before. \",\"assistant\",[96]],[\"s15\",\"d2\",1130,1465,\"Engaging in such classes can transform your view of cooking from a chore into an enjoyable and fulfilling activity, much like a form of art where you can express creativity. Additionally, many participants find that cooking together leads to meaningful conversations, where people bond over their shared love for culinary exploration. \",\"assistant\",[96]],[\"s16\",\"d2\",1465,1744,\"Trying out new recipes and techniques can even spark inspiration for meal planning, making weeknight dinners less repetitive and more exciting. Overall, it’s heartwarming to see how cooking brings people together and fosters connections through creativity and shared experiences.\",\"assistant\",[96]],[\"s17\",\"d3\",0,101,\"Conversation message indices 111–112 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s18\",\"d3\",101,107,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s19\",\"d3\",107,464,\"User: I really enjoy experimenting with different cuisines, and I recently made a traditional dish from another culture, which was quite an intriguing experience. The colorful ingredients and the rich history behind each recipe often inspire me to learn more about the culture itself, broadening my culinary horizons and appreciation for global traditions. \",\"user\",[111]],[\"s20\",\"d3\",464,817,\"It's fascinating to think about how food serves as a bridge between people, fostering connections and understanding through shared meals and flavors. I often find that every dish tells a story of its origins, the journeys of the ingredients, and the techniques passed down through generations, making cooking a deeply personal and cultural exploration. \",\"user\",[111]],[\"s21\",\"d3\",817,997,\"This experience was particularly enlightening because I learned not only about the ingredients but also about the customs and rituals that accompany the dish in its native context.\",\"user\",[111]],[\"s22\",\"d3\",997,1010,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s23\",\"d3\",1010,1394,\"Assistant: That sounds exciting! Exploring international cuisines can lead to some amazing discoveries, enriching our lives in ways that go beyond mere nourishment. The act of preparing a traditional dish not only showcases the unique flavors and cooking methods of a different culture, but it also opens a window to understanding the very essence of those people and their heritage. \",\"assistant\",[112]],[\"s24\",\"d3\",1394,1599,\"Each cuisine carries its own peculiar customs, stories, and even seasonal significance, often intertwined with festivals, celebrations, or familial gatherings, which enhances the experience of cooking it. \",\"assistant\",[112]],[\"s25\",\"d3\",1599,1971,\"Moreover, in many cultures, the preparation of food is a communal activity that brings friends and family together, allowing for the sharing of not just ingredients but also emotions, laughter, and love. It’s interesting how culinary practices can evolve over time, adapting to the availability of local ingredients while still preserving the spirit of the original dish. \",\"assistant\",[112]],[\"s26\",\"d3\",1971,2326,\"Embracing these food adventures can foster a deeper sense of connection to the world and encourage a more inclusive perspective on diverse cultural practices. I'm curious to hear which dish you made, as it might reveal even more about the culture that inspired it and, perhaps, encourage a few more tantalizing ideas for your future culinary explorations.\",\"assistant\",[112]],[\"s36\",\"d4\",0,101,\"Conversation message indices 115–116 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s37\",\"d4\",101,107,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s38\",\"d4\",107,503,\"User: I also watched a cooking show, but I found it unengaging; I much prefer hands-on experiences. The imagery and techniques presented may have been appealing, but they lacked the personal touch that comes with actually cooking for myself. There’s something incredibly rewarding about selecting fresh ingredients, measuring them precisely, and then seeing them transform into a delicious dish. \",\"user\",[115]],[\"s39\",\"d4\",503,838,\"Watching someone else cook, no matter how talented they are, can't replicate the joy of feeling the textures of this food in my hands and tasting the flavors as they meld together. Moreover, in a hands-on experience, I can experiment and adjust flavors to suit my personal preferences, something that a static show simply cannot offer.\",\"user\",[115]],[\"s40\",\"d4\",838,851,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s41\",\"d4\",851,1092,\"Assistant: That makes sense. Nothing beats the joy of cooking yourself! When you are actively engaged in the cooking process, each step becomes an opportunity for learning and creativity, allowing you to truly express yourself through food. \",\"assistant\",[116]],[\"s42\",\"d4\",1092,1442,\"The aromas that fill the kitchen as you sauté vegetables or bake bread create an atmosphere of anticipation and satisfaction that watching a screen simply cannot capture. Plus, there’s that delightful moment when you taste your creation for the first time; the excitement of discovering whether the flavors harmonize as you intended is unparalleled. \",\"assistant\",[116]],[\"s43\",\"d4\",1442,1764,\"Engaging in hands-on cooking also allows for the serendipity of experimentation, where you might stumble upon an unexpected flavor combination that delights and surprises you. Each dish becomes a personal journey, a reflection of your tastes, preferences, and even the memories associated with the ingredients you choose. \",\"assistant\",[116]],[\"s44\",\"d4\",1764,2083,\"In addition, cooking can serve as a stress-relieving activity, a way to unplug from the chaos of daily life and focus on something tactile and rewarding. It's not just about the food; it's about the experience itself, the connection to the ingredients, and the joy of creating something that can be shared with others. \",\"assistant\",[116]],[\"s45\",\"d4\",2083,2206,\"Overall, there's a profound sense of accomplishment that comes with cooking that can't be matched by passive viewing alone.\",\"assistant\",[116]],[\"s46\",\"d5\",0,101,\"Conversation message indices 153–154 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s47\",\"d5\",101,107,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s48\",\"d5\",107,450,\"User: I also opted out of cooking classes that I once enjoyed. Initially, these classes were a delightful escape for me, allowing me to explore new recipes and techniques in a supportive environment. However, over time, the atmosphere transformed significantly; it became more about competition and performance rather than the joy of cooking. \",\"user\",[153]],[\"s49\",\"d5\",450,734,\"The pressure to excel and impress the instructor took away from the simple pleasure of being in the kitchen, experimenting with flavors and ingredients. It became clear that the experience was no longer fulfilling, and I decided it was best to step back from those classes altogether.\",\"user\",[153]],[\"s50\",\"d5\",734,747,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s51\",\"d5\",747,1111,\"Assistant: That’s understandable, and I'm glad you're recognizing what environment works best for you. When we engage in activities that are meant to be enjoyable, the last thing we want is for them to become sources of stress or anxiety. It's particularly important to prioritize comfort and relaxation, especially in contexts as intimate and personal as dating. \",\"assistant\",[154]],[\"s52\",\"d5\",1111,1426,\"The shift in focus to café meetings is a wonderful choice, as these settings often allow for more organic conversations and genuine connections. The casual vibe of a café usually fosters a sense of ease, where the pressures of formal setups are stripped away, allowing individuals the space to truly be themselves. \",\"assistant\",[154]],[\"s53\",\"d5\",1426,1746,\"Plus, there's something inherently comforting about sipping a warm beverage while engaging in meaningful dialogue amidst the soft sounds of a lively café. Perhaps you have a favorite spot in mind that embodies this relaxed atmosphere. How do you feel about the dynamics of café meetings compared to previous experiences?\",\"assistant\",[154]],[\"s54\",\"d6\",0,99,\"Conversation message indices 69–70 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s55\",\"d6\",99,105,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s56\",\"d6\",105,458,\"User: I joined a couples cooking workshop to explore new cuisines and meet new people. It was really enjoyable! The atmosphere in the kitchen was vibrant, filled with the aroma of spices and fresh ingredients. We were all eagerly trying to follow the chef's instructions, which made for some entertaining moments when things didn’t quite go as planned. \",\"user\",[69]],[\"s57\",\"d6\",458,745,\"I particularly loved the way everyone seemed to open up and share stories about their culinary experiences, which truly made it feel like a communal gathering rather than a formal class. Overall, it was a delightful way to blend learning with social interaction and culinary exploration.\",\"user\",[69]],[\"s58\",\"d6\",745,758,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]]],\"required_scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"past\"},\"rules_completion_required\":false,\"supported_task_outputs\":{},\"personal_response_selection\":true,\"phase\":\"history_evidence\",\"source_documents\":{\"d1\":{\"doc_id\":\"personamem:memory:e532164f4811a26fc503ea6a47fe72f69d8aca1fb4c475c408ffa60f0908d856\",\"time_metadata\":{\"observed_start\":93.0,\"observed_end\":94.0,\"event_start\":null,\"event_end\":null,\"validity\":\"unknown\",\"time_source\":\"message_index\"}},\"d2\":{\"doc_id\":\"personamem:memory:71daa07ec7d9fdbaf4a830ba8fdead8251507af7b9c014fe4ca59aa70488ff0b\",\"time_metadata\":{\"observed_start\":95.0,\"observed_end\":96.0,\"event_start\":null,\"event_end\":null,\"validity\":\"unknown\",\"time_source\":\"message_index\"}},\"d3\":{\"doc_id\":\"personamem:memory:689b6359490a8979cfbb2777b9c651933c2fc330de14aa815771033577363553\",\"time_metadata\":{\"observed_start\":111.0,\"observed_end\":112.0,\"event_start\":null,\"event_end\":null,\"validity\":\"unknown\",\"time_source\":\"message_index\"}},\"d4\":{\"doc_id\":\"personamem:memory:94f5c18864a1a2b8ac26c86c3a9c39d2dbf52eebde86c3ef1e551cc891bf2561\",\"time_metadata\":{\"observed_start\":115.0,\"observed_end\":116.0,\"event_start\":n